  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remnants of Hurricane Isaias to bring rain, \nbreezy conditions Sunday into Sunday night.\n\nThe main weather impact to the region over the next week is going to \nbe from the remnants of what is now Hurricane Isaias. Model guidance \nis in generally good agreement that Hurricane Isaias' surface low \nweakens and broadens as the system moves into the TN Valley Saturday \nnight into Sunday. The mid-level circulation amplifies and becomes a \nstrong cutoff low over KY/TN that moves slowly east across the Mid-\nAtlantic Sunday into Monday. Periods of rain are likely across the \narea at times Saturday evening through Sunday night. Rainfall \namounts of 1-2 inches are expected across the area. Higher rain \ntotals are certainly possible, but will depend on a few other \nsmaller scale features. \n\nTropical moisture surges north into our area Saturday night into \nSunday, with the highest PWAT values at or above 2\" (most likely \neast of the Blue Ridge). While the main cutoff low associated with \nthe remnants of Isaias remain west of the Appalachians on Saturday, \nexpect light rain to move in from south to north in the evening. \nForcing for ascent increases Saturday night, and especially Sunday \nmorning into Sunday afternoon as mid-level heights fall with the \napproach of the cutoff low. There is going to be enhanced low-level \nconvergence somewhere over the region as the surface high to the \neast and cutoff low to the west interact. Easterly flow into the \nBlue Ridge could also produce enhanced rain amounts due to \norographic effects. Additionally, an inverted trough or wave of low \npressure tries to develop in lee of the Appalachians Sunday as it \ncrosses Virginia to the Delmarva.\n\nUncertainty remains regarding the exact evolution and timing of \nthese features, which will ultimately determine where / if any \nlocally heavy rain occurs. Continue to monitor the forecast over the \nnext couple of days. No Flood Watches are planned at this time as we \nare still over 48 hours from the main slug of rain. \n\nIt is going to be noticeably breezy, especially in the mountains \nwhere winds begin to pick up Saturday evening. The strongest winds \nare most likely on Sunday. The current forecast for peak wind gusts \nis 30-40 mph in the Alleghenies and 20-30 mph elsewhere.\n\nConditions dry out Monday morning into Monday afternoon as the \nremnants of Isaias push offshore. Looking at mostly mild \ntemperatures for most of next week. A series of cold fronts cross \nthe region during the middle to late part of next week as the \nsynoptic pattern becomes more active. Aside from mountain rain \nshowers, most of the area looks to remain dry. The strongest cold \nfront arrives late next week, possibly bringing rain and colder air \nbehind it.",
